0
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,290


1
00:00:03,290 --> 00:00:06,230
One of our classic problems to analyze

2
00:00:06,230 --> 00:00:08,320
using Newton's second law is the motion

3
00:00:08,320 --> 00:00:11,670
of two blocks with a rope that's wrapped around a pulley.

4
00:00:11,670 --> 00:00:16,990
So imagine we have a pulley, P, and we hang from that block 1

5
00:00:16,990 --> 00:00:19,360
and we hang from that block 2.

6
00:00:19,360 --> 00:00:22,480
The pulley is suspended by another rope,

7
00:00:22,480 --> 00:00:25,180
and it's attached to a wall.

8
00:00:25,180 --> 00:00:28,150
And let's now try to use Newton's second law

9
00:00:28,150 --> 00:00:30,040
to analyze this motion.

10
00:00:30,040 --> 00:00:33,450
Now, going back to our methodology, the first thing

11
00:00:33,450 --> 00:00:36,320
we want to ask ourselves is to identify

12
00:00:36,320 --> 00:00:39,190
what are all the moving pieces?

13
00:00:39,190 --> 00:00:44,290
So I'm going to make my first assumption here,

14
00:00:44,290 --> 00:00:51,020
that the rope/pulley a surface so that's right

15
00:00:51,020 --> 00:00:55,070
here is frictionless.

16
00:00:55,070 --> 00:00:59,170
Now, what that assumption means is that the pulley

17
00:00:59,170 --> 00:01:01,030
will remain at rest.

18
00:01:01,030 --> 00:01:03,840


19
00:01:03,840 --> 00:01:07,130
And therefore, when we want to break this problem down,

20
00:01:07,130 --> 00:01:13,939
our first question is to identify all the moving

21
00:01:13,939 --> 00:01:14,438
objects.

22
00:01:14,438 --> 00:01:17,580


23
00:01:17,580 --> 00:01:20,150
And so we really have three.

24
00:01:20,150 --> 00:01:26,490
We have mass, which we label by 1, 2, and the rope.

25
00:01:26,490 --> 00:01:30,380
So our goal now is to identify all the moving objects

26
00:01:30,380 --> 00:01:42,990
and to draw free body force diagrams for each moving

27
00:01:42,990 --> 00:01:43,490
object.

28
00:01:43,490 --> 00:01:47,290


29
00:01:47,290 --> 00:01:49,040
Now, one of the things that I'm especially

30
00:01:49,040 --> 00:01:54,890
interested in talking about is to keep in mind to identify

31
00:01:54,890 --> 00:01:57,290
action-reaction pairs.

32
00:01:57,290 --> 00:02:01,120
So as I go through and draw these free body diagrams,

33
00:02:01,120 --> 00:02:03,950
we want to ask ourselves, what forces

34
00:02:03,950 --> 00:02:07,090
form Newton's third law action-reaction pairs?

35
00:02:07,090 --> 00:02:11,590
So what I'll do is I'll start with object 1.

36
00:02:11,590 --> 00:02:16,100
Now, I'm object 1 has a gravitational force M 1 g.

37
00:02:16,100 --> 00:02:19,980
And the rope is pulling object 1 up

38
00:02:19,980 --> 00:02:24,560
with the tension at the end of the rope T rope 1.

39
00:02:24,560 --> 00:02:28,160
So the action- reaction pair to M 1 g

40
00:02:28,160 --> 00:02:30,990
is the Earth, is the force of this mass on the Earth,

41
00:02:30,990 --> 00:02:32,430
which we're not considering.

42
00:02:32,430 --> 00:02:34,350
What about the rope?

43
00:02:34,350 --> 00:02:39,550
So now, let's draw a picture of our rope.

44
00:02:39,550 --> 00:02:45,250
And mass, this object here is pulling the rope down,

45
00:02:45,250 --> 00:02:50,190
and so we have, on the rope, object 1 pulling it down.

46
00:02:50,190 --> 00:02:56,520
And this is our action-reaction pair.

47
00:02:56,520 --> 00:02:58,670
What are the other forces on the rope?

48
00:02:58,670 --> 00:03:08,040
Well over here, object 2, t 2, is pulling the rope down

49
00:03:08,040 --> 00:03:11,640
with the tension at the end of that rope.

50
00:03:11,640 --> 00:03:15,460
Now, also, the pulley is exerting a force

51
00:03:15,460 --> 00:03:19,710
on the rope upwards, because we have that force.

52
00:03:19,710 --> 00:03:23,300
And so these are the force diagrams on the rope.

53
00:03:23,300 --> 00:03:26,090
Now, what about the action-reaction pair

54
00:03:26,090 --> 00:03:27,770
to T 2 rope?

55
00:03:27,770 --> 00:03:31,100
Well, let's continue and draw 2.

56
00:03:31,100 --> 00:03:33,720
We have, again, gravitational force on 2,

57
00:03:33,720 --> 00:03:35,730
the Earth is the action-reaction pair.

58
00:03:35,730 --> 00:03:39,360
The force of 2 on the Earth upwards

59
00:03:39,360 --> 00:03:42,117
is equal to m 2g of the Earth downwards.

60
00:03:42,117 --> 00:03:43,950
We're not drawing the Earth in this picture,

61
00:03:43,950 --> 00:03:44,783
so we don't show it.

62
00:03:44,783 --> 00:03:50,150
Now, here is the force of the rope on 2.

63
00:03:50,150 --> 00:03:54,670
And there is our other action-reaction pair.

64
00:03:54,670 --> 00:03:58,290
So these are Newton's third law pairs in this object.

65
00:03:58,290 --> 00:04:01,580
Now, now that we've identified all the forces,

66
00:04:01,580 --> 00:04:05,290
we want to apply Newton's second law

67
00:04:05,290 --> 00:04:11,020
to each of these objects that are moving.

68
00:04:11,020 --> 00:04:16,265
So let's begin by remembering that if our rope--

69
00:04:16,265 --> 00:04:20,390
our next assumption here, it's assumed

70
00:04:20,390 --> 00:04:23,610
that the mass of the rope is very light.

71
00:04:23,610 --> 00:04:26,980
And then the tension in the rope is constant.

72
00:04:26,980 --> 00:04:32,290
And the implication of that is T 2 r equals t 1 r.

73
00:04:32,290 --> 00:04:38,200
And that's why we can now identify this as T

74
00:04:38,200 --> 00:04:41,760
and that as T.

75
00:04:41,760 --> 00:04:44,320
Little bit later on, when we talk about pulleys

76
00:04:44,320 --> 00:04:47,450
that have mass with friction between them,

77
00:04:47,450 --> 00:04:49,950
the tension on the two sides of the pulley

78
00:04:49,950 --> 00:04:51,570
will not be the same.

79
00:04:51,570 --> 00:04:53,830
But for the moment, we've made these assumptions,

80
00:04:53,830 --> 00:04:55,120
and that holds.

81
00:04:55,120 --> 00:04:59,900
And now, we just really have objects 1 and object 2 to analyze.

82
00:04:59,900 --> 00:05:03,490
And so one of the things that helps a lot

83
00:05:03,490 --> 00:05:07,010
is, we need to choose unit vectors in order

84
00:05:07,010 --> 00:05:09,170
to write down the vector equations for Newton's

85
00:05:09,170 --> 00:05:10,140
second law.

86
00:05:10,140 --> 00:05:13,420
So let's just here assume for ourselves

87
00:05:13,420 --> 00:05:16,170
that M 1 is greater than M 2.

88
00:05:16,170 --> 00:05:18,490
This gives us some feeling for how the system moves.

89
00:05:18,490 --> 00:05:20,110
I like to do this because it gives me

90
00:05:20,110 --> 00:05:22,390
a way to choose my unit vectors to make

91
00:05:22,390 --> 00:05:24,910
all the accelerations positive.

92
00:05:24,910 --> 00:05:29,570
So when M 1 is bigger than M 2, I expect M 1 to go down

93
00:05:29,570 --> 00:05:31,170
and 2 go up.

94
00:05:31,170 --> 00:05:35,780
And that's how I'm going to choose unit vectors j hat 1.

95
00:05:35,780 --> 00:05:38,820
Now, here is the very interesting thing.

96
00:05:38,820 --> 00:05:42,870
We choose a separate coordinate system for each object.

97
00:05:42,870 --> 00:05:44,680
So I'm going to write that down.

98
00:05:44,680 --> 00:05:57,140
We want to choose separate unit vectors for each object.

99
00:05:57,140 --> 00:05:59,550
And our accelerations will be with respect

100
00:05:59,550 --> 00:06:01,620
to those unit vectors.

101
00:06:01,620 --> 00:06:04,060
This is where a lot of people get tripped up.

102
00:06:04,060 --> 00:06:07,800
So here, I'm choosing j hat 2.

103
00:06:07,800 --> 00:06:12,680
And when I choose that, I expect a 2

104
00:06:12,680 --> 00:06:15,350
to be positive because 2 is going up

105
00:06:15,350 --> 00:06:16,730
in the direction of j hat.

106
00:06:16,730 --> 00:06:23,680
And here, I expect a 1 to be positive also,

107
00:06:23,680 --> 00:06:26,290
because 1 is going down.

108
00:06:26,290 --> 00:06:31,250
Now I can draw Newton's second law on 1.

109
00:06:31,250 --> 00:06:37,500
So I have F 1 equals M 1 a 1.

110
00:06:37,500 --> 00:06:41,970
And now I analyze my forces, M 1 g is in the positive j hat

111
00:06:41,970 --> 00:06:45,040
direction, t is in the negative j hat direction,

112
00:06:45,040 --> 00:06:50,440
so I have M 1 g minus t equals M 1 a 1,

113
00:06:50,440 --> 00:06:52,770
which I'm expecting to be positive.

114
00:06:52,770 --> 00:07:01,260
And in the same way, on 2, F 2 equals M 2 a 2.

115
00:07:01,260 --> 00:07:02,510
I look at my force diagram.

116
00:07:02,510 --> 00:07:07,210
Now, notice, j hat is up, so t is positive

117
00:07:07,210 --> 00:07:15,170
and 2 g is minus my direction, and 2 g equals M 2 a 2.

118
00:07:15,170 --> 00:07:19,620
So I now have two equations, but when I look at these equations,

119
00:07:19,620 --> 00:07:21,880
I see that I have three unknowns.

120
00:07:21,880 --> 00:07:25,420
I have t, a 1, and a 2.

121
00:07:25,420 --> 00:07:29,090
But I have a constraint, because these objects are moving

122
00:07:29,090 --> 00:07:33,070
together and the way I've chosen the coordinate systems,

123
00:07:33,070 --> 00:07:40,440
a 1 is equal to plus a 2, both are positive with respect

124
00:07:40,440 --> 00:07:42,720
to my choice of coordinate systems.

125
00:07:42,720 --> 00:07:45,910
And so these two as are the same.

126
00:07:45,910 --> 00:07:50,165
And now I can solve my equations for t and a.

127
00:07:50,165 --> 00:07:51,960
And let's quickly do that.

128
00:07:51,960 --> 00:07:56,006
M 1 g minus t equals M 1 a.

129
00:07:56,006 --> 00:07:59,790
And over here, let's solve for the tension,

130
00:07:59,790 --> 00:08:01,530
for the accelerations here.

131
00:08:01,530 --> 00:08:06,860
T is equal to m 2 a plus m 2 g.

132
00:08:06,860 --> 00:08:10,330
And when I substitute the t into that equation,

133
00:08:10,330 --> 00:08:12,770
we'll find some space for that.

134
00:08:12,770 --> 00:08:20,470
we'll write that as, if I put in the M 2 minus M 2 a minus M 2 g

135
00:08:20,470 --> 00:08:24,220
and bring the M 2 a to the other side,

136
00:08:24,220 --> 00:08:26,350
then it's a little bit of algebra,

137
00:08:26,350 --> 00:08:32,362
but I think you'll trust me that this is M 1 minus M 2

138
00:08:32,362 --> 00:08:40,200
g over M 1 plus M 2.

139
00:08:40,200 --> 00:08:42,640
Now, we're almost done.

140
00:08:42,640 --> 00:08:44,340
We need to check our answers.

141
00:08:44,340 --> 00:08:47,330
First off, does it have the dimensions of acceleration?

142
00:08:47,330 --> 00:08:49,990
Answer, yes, mass divided by mass,

143
00:08:49,990 --> 00:08:52,170
g has the dimensions of acceleration.

144
00:08:52,170 --> 00:08:55,270
So that's my first check always with my algebra.

145
00:08:55,270 --> 00:08:59,430
Second check, what if M 1 is equal to M 2?

146
00:08:59,430 --> 00:09:03,830
Then the acceleration is 0, I expect them to be balanced.

147
00:09:03,830 --> 00:09:06,530
I've said that M 1 is greater than M 2.

148
00:09:06,530 --> 00:09:09,200
Positive sign, my A is positive.

149
00:09:09,200 --> 00:09:11,120
That's how I set up my coordinate system,

150
00:09:11,120 --> 00:09:12,500
so I expect that too.

151
00:09:12,500 --> 00:09:16,630
By the way, our symbol a was equal to those.

152
00:09:16,630 --> 00:09:21,380
And so I'm pretty confident that my result is correct.

153
00:09:21,380 --> 00:09:23,740
And that's how we apply Newton's second law

154
00:09:23,740 --> 00:09:27,450
to the pulley with several assumptions,

155
00:09:27,450 --> 00:09:31,210
frictionless surface, massless rope.

156
00:09:31,210 --> 00:09:36,290
And think about how we chose our unit vectors.

157
00:09:36,290 --> 00:09:40,030
Each object gets its separate coordinate system.

158
00:09:40,030 --> 00:09:42,030
I'm not using the same coordinate system

159
00:09:42,030 --> 00:09:43,842
for each object.

160
00:09:43,842 --> 00:09:45,300
I could've, but then there would be

161
00:09:45,300 --> 00:09:48,150
a subtlety to this condition, the accelerations

162
00:09:48,150 --> 00:09:51,052
would be opposite in sign.

163
00:09:51,052 --> 00:09:51,552


